+### 6.10 Attribution of the retrieval gain on development data
+
+> **Development data only (OV-dev-A, OV-dev-B, XM-dev500): DEVELOPMENT / C-ATTR-DEV, NON-CONFIRMATORY.** This study was registered after every test set of this paper had been decided. It changes no verdict of Sections 6.2–6.9 or 8 and enters neither the abstract nor the contributions. OV-dev-A and OV-dev-B are re-used development sets on which the retrieval contrast τ_sel was already known (Section 5.7).
+
+**6.10.1 What ran.** Block 1 decoded the `perm`, `fix` and extra `rand` arms, and Aya-Vision-8B's `zs`, `ret` and `rand` d0 on XM-dev500, in batches, as registered; it ended with all its registered steps complete, in 6.41 GPU-hours of its 12-hour cap, and carries no registered claim (Section 6.10.2). Block 1b re-decoded every arm that enters an attribution contrast at batch size 1 under the hard cap of 17.95 GPU-hours fixed after D1, and used 12.27 GPU-hours. All 50 registered batch-size-1 runs (Tiers A, B and C) finished, with no cap stop and no job stop. Scoring followed the registered order (Section 5.7); its verification stage passed, with the per-image prompt hashes of 50 batch-size-1 runs compared in 50 pairs and 0 unequal pairs, and the scoring parity gate passed (ok).
+
+#### 6.10.2 Batch composition changed greedy captions (kill gate K1)
+
+**What was registered.** The attribution registration assumed that one image's caption does not depend on the other images decoded in the same batch (assumption A1). Before any attribution arm was used, each backbone re-decoded the first 50 OV-dev-B items of its stored `ret` run twice on the new machine: (a) in the stored order and batch slices, compared with the stored captions (host parity; also on the first 50 OV-dev-A items for Qwen2.5-VL-7B and InternVL3-8B); and (b) in reversed order at batch size 1, compared with the same machine's decode (a). Each comparison prints only the number of identical strings and the ids of the others. A pass needs all 50. A failure of (b) was kill gate K1: the backbone leaves the registered families, and its rows are printed "A1 not met".
+
+**What happened.** Host parity held for every backbone and set checked (Table S20). Check (b) failed for all three backbones: 42/50 captions were identical for Qwen2.5-VL-7B, 37/50 for InternVL3-8B and 31/50 for Aya-Vision-8B. The registered report sentence applies: "K1 fired: no backbone met A1 under registration v1.1; block 1 as registered carries no claim." No nomination rule was computed on the batched decodes; a nomination file with zero claims and the reason K1 was written, and its sha256 logged, before any score existed.
+
+**What it means.** The decoder is greedy (one beam, no sampling), runs in bf16 with each model's own processor, and pads every batch on the left to the length of its longest prompt. Check (a) shows that a decode is reproducible given its batch: the same images in the same slices on the new machine gave the stored strings. Check (b) shows that it is not invariant to the batch: for these three models, 8, 13 and 19 of 50 greedy captions changed (Qwen2.5-VL-7B, InternVL3-8B, Aya-Vision-8B) when the same images were decoded one at a time. The determinism check D1 below decoded the same 50 items at batch size 1 in the stored order and found every string equal to the reversed batch-size-1 decode, so at batch size 1 the order of the items changed none of these captions; the differences in (b) go with the change of batch composition, not with the change of order. A reading of the decoder code, made after K1 fired and not tested here, is that batch-mates change the padding and the shape of the numerical work, and that small numerical differences can flip a greedy choice. In this study the arms differ by their example blocks, so their prompts have different lengths and different padding; a per-image difference between two arms decoded in batches can therefore contain a batch-mate term that differs by arm. Fixed batch slices do not remove it, and an image bootstrap does not see the coupling of images within a slice.
+
+**What it does not show.** No score was compared: K1 rests on string identity only. The check says nothing about the quality of either caption, about the sign or size of any score difference, or about whether batched or one-at-a-time decoding gives better captions; it does not show that any batched result of this paper is biased. It does not identify the numerical cause. It covers these three models, this software stack and 50 OV-dev-B items each.
+
+**Consequences for this paper.** Every registered contrast of this paper between two text-example arms of one backbone (stages A and B, the Vietnamese and English tests, the Flickr30K registration) was decoded in fixed batches, with the same batch policy for both arms. The LMCap re-implementation and the interleaved image-caption arm were decoded one query at a time, so their contrasts with a backbone's `ret` (Tables 13, 14 and S19) set a decode at batch size 1 beside a batched one. All these rows are results of the systems as run; none of their numbers changes, and none is relabelled. The decode-parity checks reported earlier as 52/52 identical captions re-decoded the same items in the same slices on the same stack: they show run-to-run parity, not batch invariance (Section 9.1 and Box 2 are corrected accordingly).
+
+**Consequences for the attribution study.** After K1 fired, and before any of its outputs was scored or opened, an addendum to the registration (version 1.2) changed the batch size and the A1 check, with what follows from them (Section 5.7). Every arm that enters an attribution contrast was decoded anew at batch size 1, with the same prompts (per-image prompt hashes compared with the batched or stored run of the same cell), the same model snapshots and the same environment; all arms of one backbone and set came from one machine. A1 holds by construction at batch size 1 once the decode is deterministic, so the addendum replaced A1 by a determinism check, D1: the first 50 OV-dev-B `ret` items decoded at batch size 1 in the stored order must equal, as strings, the reversed batch-size-1 decode of check (b). D1 passed for all three backbones (50/50, 50/50, 50/50). Every attribution row in Tables 15 and 16 and in Tables S21 and S23(a) comes from batch-size-1 decoding and carries the registered label "batch size 1; registered in v1.2 after K1 fired, on string-identity counts only". The batched block-1 rows are printed only as descriptive rows, each labelled "A1 not met; batched decode; descriptive": the sensitivity row of Table S22, the fixed-set rows of Table S23(b) and Aya-Vision-8B's XM-dev500 row of Table 17. On the post hoc reading above, their contrast between `ret` and `perm` is best read as the effect of switching a whole fixed-slice decoding policy rather than as a per-image effect; this reading was not tested.
